---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import PaceCalculator from '@components/islands/PaceCalculator.astro';
import MultipointPaceCalculator from '@components/islands/MultipointPaceCalculator.astro';
import FinishTimeCalculator from '@components/islands/FinishTimeCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('health', 'pace-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How do I calculate running pace?', answer: 'Pace is your time divided by distance. Enter the distance you ran and your elapsed time, and the calculator shows your pace per kilometre and per mile, your speed, and equivalent finish times for common distances at the same pace. For example, 5 km in 25 minutes is a pace of 5:00 per kilometre.' },
  { question: 'What is a good running pace?', answer: 'It depends on fitness and distance. Many beginners run around 7–8 min/km (11–13 min/mile), and regular recreational runners average 5–6 min/km (8–10 min/mile). The best benchmark is your own trend over time, not someone else\'s number.' },
  { question: 'How do I pace myself when running?', answer: 'Start slightly slower than your goal pace and aim for even or negative splits — the second half as fast as or faster than the first. Going out too hard is the most common reason runs fall apart; a steady effort almost always yields a better finish time.' },
  { question: 'What is a pace run?', answer: 'A pace run (or tempo run) is a sustained effort at or near your goal race pace, used to train your body to hold that speed comfortably. It sits between easy running and hard intervals in intensity.' },
  { question: 'How are the equivalent finish times calculated?', answer: 'The calculator applies your current pace evenly across each distance — these are equivalent times at the same pace, not race predictions. Real race times rise slightly over longer distances as fatigue sets in, so treat a marathon time projected from a 5K pace as optimistic.' },
  { question: 'Can I work out a distance or a time instead of a pace?', answer: 'Yes. Choose Pace, Time or Distance under Calculate and fill in the other two. The box being worked out locks so it cannot be both the question and the answer, and the result is written back into it afterwards so you can switch target and carry it straight into the next calculation.' },
  { question: 'What is the Multipoint Pace Calculator for?', answer: 'It shows how your pace changed across a run rather than averaging it away. Enter the distance and the clock time at several points — as your watch reads them at a marker — and it gives the pace of each leg between them alongside your running average. That is how you see whether you went out too fast or finished strong.' },
  { question: 'How does the Finish Time Calculator work?', answer: 'It takes the distance you have covered, the time on the clock and the total race distance, works out the pace you have held, and projects that pace over the whole distance. It also shows how far and how long is left, which is usually the number that decides whether you push or ease off.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  rail
  intro="Work out any one of time, distance and pace from the other two — plus split-by-split pacing and a mid-race finish-time projection."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-28' }}
>
  <PaceCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <MultipointPaceCalculator />
    <FinishTimeCalculator />

    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose which of <strong>Pace</strong>, <strong>Time</strong> or <strong>Distance</strong> you want worked out. That box locks, because it is the answer rather than a question.</li>
      <li>Fill in the other two. Distance and pace each carry their own unit, so a 10 km run can be paced per mile.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The answer appears in the panel and is written back into its own box, so you can switch target and carry it into the next sum.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Two more tools sit above. The <strong>Multipoint Pace Calculator</strong> takes the distance
      and clock time at several points and works out what each leg cost you as well as your average
      to that point — the difference between "I ran a 25-minute 5K" and "I went out too fast". The
      <strong>Finish Time Calculator</strong> answers the question you ask yourself mid-race: at
      this pace, what will the clock say at the line, and how much is left?
    </p>

    <h2>Pace versus speed</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Pace</strong> is how long it takes to cover one unit of distance — minutes per
      kilometre or per mile — and it is how most runners think about effort. <strong>Speed</strong>
      is the inverse: distance covered per hour. A pace of 5:00 per kilometre and a speed of 12 km/h
      describe exactly the same run, just from opposite directions.
    </p>

    <h2>The formula</h2>
    <p>Pace and speed come straight from distance and time:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pace</strong> = time ÷ distance (e.g. seconds per kilometre)</li>
      <li><strong>Speed</strong> = distance ÷ time (e.g. kilometres per hour)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The equivalent finish times apply your pace evenly across each race distance, so they show what you would run <em>if</em> you held the same pace throughout.</p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Run <strong>5 km in 25 minutes</strong> and your pace is 1,500 seconds ÷ 5 = 300 seconds, or
      <strong>5:00 per kilometre</strong> (about <strong>8:03 per mile</strong>). Your speed is
      <strong>12 km/h</strong> (about 7.46 mph). Held evenly, that pace projects to:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Distance</th><th>Equivalent time at 5:00 /km</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>5K</td><td>25:00</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10K</td><td>50:00</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Half marathon</td><td>1:45:29</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Marathon</td><td>3:30:58</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>What counts as a good pace</h2>
    <p>
      There is no universal benchmark — pace depends on distance, terrain and experience. Beginners
      often run 7–8 min/km (11–13 min/mile), while seasoned recreational runners hold 5–6 min/km
      (8–10 min/mile) over a 5K. Rather than compare with others, watch your own pace drift downward
      as fitness builds; that trend is the real measure of progress.
    </p>

    <h2>Pacing yourself well</h2>
    <p>
      The most common racing mistake is starting too fast. Aim to run the first half slightly
      conservative and finish as strong as or stronger than you started — a "negative split." Even
      effort, not a fast start, almost always produces your best finish time.
    </p>

    <h2>Using pace in training</h2>
    <p>
      Knowing your pace keeps easy days genuinely easy and hard efforts controlled. Because the
      equivalent times assume you hold one pace for the whole distance, treat a marathon time
      projected from a 5K as optimistic — real races slow over distance as fatigue sets in. Pair
      pace with the <a href="/health/target-heart-rate-calculator">target heart rate calculator</a>
      to judge intensity from two angles. This is general information, not coaching or medical advice.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
